19Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27098911410", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "International organization that represents chemists in individual countries Headquartered in Zurich. Founded 1919.", "newValue": "The International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry (IUPAC) is a pivotal entity in the world of chemistry, established in 1919 to unify and represent chemists across nations. Headquartered in Zurich, Switzerland, IUPAC emerged from the need for standardization in chemical nomenclature and measurement, a necessity underscored by the explosion of chemical innovations in the early 20th century. Prominent figures such as Hermann Staudinger, who was influential in polymer chemistry, and other leading chemists from various countries played crucial roles in its formation, emphasizing its global significance in the scientific community.

